      <div class="prose max-w-none text-slate-600 mb-6">
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
          A modern digital computer is an electronic information-processing system governed by stored instructions. Below are the four essential operational characteristics along with an evolutionary progression of core hardware technologies across all five computer generations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 4 Primary Characteristics Infographic Cards -->
      <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-sky-600" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/></svg>
        Four Primary Characteristics of Modern Digital Computers
      </h3>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-8">
        <!-- Speed -->
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-sky-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-sky-100 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute -right-3 -bottom-3 text-sky-200 opacity-40">
            <svg class="w-20 h-20" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 2C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10S17.52 2 12 2zm1 15h-2v-6h2v6zm0-8h-2V7h2v2z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-sky-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold mb-3 shadow-md">
            1
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-1">Phenomenal Speed</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-2">
            Executes billions of discrete instructions per second, measured in <strong>GHz, MIPS, or TFLOPS</strong>. Operations that require human lifetimes execute in picoseconds ($10^{-12}\text{s}$).
          </p>
          <span class="inline-block text-[11px] font-semibold text-sky-700 bg-sky-100/70 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">Cycle time &lt; 0.3 ns</span>
        </div>

        <!-- Accuracy -->
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-50 to-teal-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-emerald-100 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-emerald-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold mb-3 shadow-md">
            2
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-1">Uncompromising Accuracy</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-2">
            Maintains absolute precision governed by the <strong>GIGO principle</strong> (Garbage In, Garbage Out). Computational errors stem from faulty logic or imprecise inputs, never electronic fatigue.
          </p>
          <span class="inline-block text-[11px] font-semibold text-emerald-700 bg-emerald-100/70 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">IEEE-754 FP64 Standard</span>
        </div>

        <!-- Diligence -->
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-50 to-orange-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-amber-100 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-amber-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold mb-3 shadow-md">
            3
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-1">Continuous Diligence</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-2">
            Capable of continuous 24/7 computation without loss of concentration, tiredness, or degradation of accuracy. Millions of repeated matrix transformations execute identically from start to finish.
          </p>
          <span class="inline-block text-[11px] font-semibold text-amber-700 bg-amber-100/70 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">99.999% High Availability</span>
        </div>

        <!-- Versatility & Storage -->
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-purple-50 to-indigo-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-purple-100 shadow-sm relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-purple-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold mb-3 shadow-md">
            4
          </div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-1">Versatility &amp; Capacity</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-2">
            Transitions seamlessly from scientific simulations to digital rendering via program reconfigurability, backed by massive non-volatile memory hierarchies capable of addressing petabytes of data.
          </p>
          <span class="inline-block text-[11px] font-semibold text-purple-700 bg-purple-100/70 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">Von Neumann Architecture</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-indigo-600" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 8v4l3 3m6-3a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
        Evolutionary Hardware Timeline of the Five Generations
      </h3>

      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-slate-200 ml-4 pl-6 space-y-6 sm:space-y-8 my-6">
        <!-- 1st Gen -->
        <div class="relative group">
          <div class="absolute -left-[35px] top-1.5 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-slate-800 border-4 border-white shadow"></div>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 hover:border-sky-300 transition">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 mb-1.5">
              <span class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base">1st Generation (1940 – 1956): Thermionic Vacuum Tubes</span>
              <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs bg-slate-200 text-slate-700 font-mono">ENIAC, EDVAC, UNIVAC I</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600">
              <strong>Core Hardware:</strong> Thermionic Valves / Vacuum Tubes (~18,000 tubes in ENIAC) for logic switching; Magnetic Drums &amp; Mercury Delay Lines for primary storage. High power consumption (~150 kW), immense heat output, frequent burnouts (MTBF &lt; 2 hours). Programmed in raw Machine Language.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 2nd Gen -->
        <div class="relative group">
          <div class="absolute -left-[35px] top-1.5 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-sky-600 border-4 border-white shadow"></div>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 hover:border-sky-300 transition">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 mb-1.5">
              <span class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base">2nd Generation (1956 – 1963): Discrete Solid-State Transistors</span>
              <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs bg-sky-100 text-sky-800 font-mono">IBM 7094, CDC 1604</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600">
              <strong>Core Hardware:</strong> Bipolar Junction Transistors (invented at Bell Labs) replaced fragile filaments. Magnetic Core Memory introduced for volatile RAM; magnetic tapes/disks for secondary storage. 10x reduction in size, drastically lowered power dissipation, Assembly and early High-Level Languages (FORTRAN, COBOL).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 3rd Gen -->
        <div class="relative group">
          <div class="absolute -left-[35px] top-1.5 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-emerald-600 border-4 border-white shadow"></div>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 hover:border-sky-300 transition">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 mb-1.5">
              <span class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base">3rd Generation (1964 – 1971): Integrated Circuits (SSI &amp; MSI)</span>
              <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 font-mono">IBM System/360, PDP-8</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600">
              <strong>Core Hardware:</strong> Small-Scale &amp; Medium-Scale Integrated Circuits (Jack Kilby &amp; Robert Noyce) fabricating dozens to hundreds of transistors onto a single silicon substrate. Emergence of keyboards, cathode-ray monitors, and Time-Sharing Operating Systems.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 4th Gen -->
        <div class="relative group">
          <div class="absolute -left-[35px] top-1.5 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-600 border-4 border-white shadow"></div>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 hover:border-sky-300 transition">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 mb-1.5">
              <span class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base">4th Generation (1971 – Present): VLSI &amp; ULSI Microprocessors</span>
              <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs bg-amber-100 text-amber-800 font-mono">Intel 4004, x86, ARM, Apple M-Series</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600">
              <strong>Core Hardware:</strong> Very Large Scale Integration (VLSI) putting 100,000+ transistors, and later Ultra Large Scale Integration (ULSI) putting billions of FinFET transistors onto a single CPU die. Semiconductor DRAM, Solid State NVMe drives, personal computers (PCs), the Internet, and ubiquitous mobile computing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 5th Gen -->
        <div class="relative group">
          <div class="absolute -left-[35px] top-1.5 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-purple-600 border-4 border-white shadow"></div>
          <div class="bg-purple-50/60 p-4 rounded-xl border border-purple-200 hover:border-purple-400 transition">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 mb-1.5">
              <span class="font-bold text-purple-950 text-sm sm:text-base">5th Generation (Present &amp; Beyond): AI Acceleration, Quantum &amp; Neuromorphic Silicon</span>
              <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-xs bg-purple-200 text-purple-900 font-mono">Tensor Processing Units, QPU, Neuromorphic Chips</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-purple-900">
              <strong>Core Hardware:</strong> Highly parallel Neural Processing Units (NPUs), Tensor Processing Units (TPUs), 3nm gate-all-around (GAA) silicon, optical interconnects, and experimental superconducting quantum bits (qubits). Focuses on massively parallel artificial intelligence inferencing, natural language synthesis, and autonomous decision pipelines.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

